preConditionHook:
// THE MOST-CITED BROOKS STOP: the initial protective stop sits stopOffsetTicks
// beyond the SIGNAL bar's extreme opposite the trade -- for a long, below the
// low of the bar immediately before the entry bar -- and it stays there for the
// life of the trade unless a tightening rule, a separate placement, replaces it.
// All five sources use one tick.
//
// THE SIGNAL BAR IS CAPTURED ONCE ON THE FILL BAR rather than re-read every bar
// at low[ctx.BarsSinceEntry() + 1]. The two are the same price -- BarsSinceEntry
// is VERIFIED as 0 on the bar the entry fills, so the signal bar is bar [1]
// there -- but the capture holds the deepest subscript at 1 instead of the
// length of the longest trade, which Max Bars Back would otherwise have to
// cover. It is the same construction StopAtSignalBarRangeMultiple and
// StopFractionOfSignalBarRisk use. While flat this re-captures every bar, which
// is harmless and is exactly what leaves the value correct on the fill bar;
// BarsSinceEntry restarts through a reversal (VERIFIED), so a reversal
// re-captures without any MarketPosition == 0 reset.
//
// NOT StopLoss (a fixed dollar amount): this is a price-action stop anchored to a
// specific bar, so its dollar risk varies trade by trade. Not
// StopFractionOfSignalBarRisk, which sits a fraction of the way from that bar's
// extreme back toward the entry; this one is BEYOND the bar. Not
// StopAtSignalBarRangeMultiple, which converts the signal bar's RANGE to dollars.
//
// Exit tests use close[0] per the standing signal-bar-close convention; intrabar
// stop fills are not modelled. Tick division-first into a local.
//
// Every scratch value is a localVariables entry, never a hook-scope
// declaration, so the hooks survive a double placement.
//
// Deepest bar touched: 1.
tickSize = ctx.MinMove() / ctx.PriceScale();
if (el_eq((double)ctx.BarsSinceEntry(), 0.0)) {
    sigLow = low[1];
    sigHigh = high[1];
}
